18: \title{An Auditable Theory Compiler for AI-Assisted Formal Mathematics\\
19: \large Technical Supplement: Finite-Record-Only Safety under Record-Gate Soundness}
20: \titlerunning{An Auditable Theory Compiler}
21: \author{Haobo Ma\inst{1} \and Wenlin Zhang\inst{2}}
22: \authorrunning{H. Ma and W. Zhang}
23: \institute{AELF PTE LTD. \email{auric@aelf.io} \and
24: National University of Singapore \email{e1327962@u.nus.edu}}
25: 
26: \begin{document}
27: \maketitle
28: 
29: \begin{abstract}
30: Formal mathematics projects increasingly use AI agents for drafting,
31: review, theorem search, and manuscript preparation.  Ungated agent output,
32: however, can create theorem-looking but content-poor edits, stale source
33: references, duplicated submission routes, and premature paper promotion.  We
34: report an auditable theory-to-paper pipeline used around a Lean-backed
35: BEDC source workspace and an automath publication workspace.  The
36: pipeline separates source development, intake packets, active paper
37: directories, AI review loops, deterministic gates, and human promotion
38: decisions.  Its central rule is that AI output is advisory: load-bearing
39: mathematical and submission claims must be tied, at the appropriate evidence
40: level, to source paths, command logs, deterministic checks, and human-approved
41: promotion.  Under record-gate soundness, the core interface theorem constructs
42: the finite publication certificates checked by the package verifier from recorded
43: gate-passage entries and explicitly submitted review surfaces; its safety claim is
44: deliberately certificate-level and finite-record only.  It proves neither Lean builds,
45: daemon behaviour, artifact semantics, nor venue acceptance.  The current package
46: then proves schema-relative finite-record certificate exactness for its six-row, path-verified
47: instantiation, while rejecting stronger readings such as external gate
48: implementation soundness, proof-assistant correctness, artifact semantic validity,
49: current execution of every gate, or venue acceptance.  Four case
50: studies illustrate the gates at the path-verified historical-snapshot level: intake
51: isolation, overlap/submitted blocking, fake-extension rejection, and
52: artifact-limitation disclosure.
53: \end{abstract}
54: 
55: \section{Problem and contribution}
56: 
57: AI assistance is useful in a formal-mathematics project only if the project can
58: distinguish proposal from evidence.  A generated theorem statement, a legal
59: piece of code, a mechanical expansion, or a lower score in a review rubric does
60: not by itself constitute mathematical discovery.  The practical publication
61: failure modes are equally structural: a route may overlap a submitted sibling,
62: an artifact status file may drift from generated counts, or an intake note may
63: accidentally become a daemon-visible manuscript.
64: 
65: This source is the technical supplement for the presentation-only contribution
66: \emph{An Auditable Theory Compiler for AI-Assisted Formal Mathematics}, whose
67: single primary artifact is the two-page source \path{submission_abstract.tex}.
68: This file is subordinate technical support, not a competing submission title.
69: The final source-level packaging convention used throughout both sources is the
70: following: the primary CICM presentation-only article is
71: \path{submission_abstract.tex}; this file, \path{main.tex}, is the selected
72: technical supplement; \path{theorem_inventory.json} is the selected bounded
73: theorem-obligation inventory; and \path{review_bundle/} is the selected exact
74: supplemental support directory.  No archival replacement is claimed in this
75: source package.  The short artifact states the workshop-facing thesis and
76: boundary; this supplement gives the finite-record definitions, theorem proofs,
77: and case-evidence tables; and the supplemental evidence directory supplies only
78: the finite records named in the bundle-dependent claims when that exact support
79: surface is uploaded with matching final digests.  This
80: file records a compact workflow pattern for avoiding these failures: an auditable
81: theory-to-paper pipeline in which AI agents propose, review, and triage, while
82: deterministic gates decide what may be treated as load-bearing evidence.  The
83: pattern is instantiated in local BEDC and automath workspaces.
84: The central mathematical/software conclusion of the supplement is a typed
85: finite-record certificate calculus for publication claims under record-gate
86: soundness.  Its core chain is Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}
87: (the finite certificate tuple and gate dispatch),
88: Definition~\ref{def:record-gate-soundness-external-interface} and
89: Proposition~\ref{prop:record-gate-soundness-external-interface-exactness} (the
90: standalone record-gate input/output and non-claim boundary),
91: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} (the six finite
92: acceptance predicates), Theorems~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract},
93: \ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, and
94: \ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} (verifier exactness and
95: non-escalation), Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} (finite
96: publication-certificate invariants from record-gate soundness),
97: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-rigidity} (the sharp bad-subrecord
98: normal form for every stronger reading),
99: Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary-axis-non-escalation-exactness} (simultaneous exactness
100: of the source-interface, implementation-soundness, artifact-semantic, and
101: theorem-ledger boundaries), and
102: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness} (the current package's
103: schema-relative finite-record instance).  Later theoremized statements about bundle shape,
104: digests, upload conditions, command boundaries, inventories, venue records, and
105: case snapshots are support-boundary audit claims: they are useful finite-record
106: consequences, but they are not mathematical invariants of independent interest
107: and do not replace the printed TeX proofs in the core chain.  The result is
108: intentionally not a proof of external implementation-level soundness:
109: Lean builds, publication-daemon behaviour, artifact semantics, and fresh command
110: runs enter the argument only when a finite certificate records the corresponding
111: evidence surface and boundary.  Root-level diagnostic files outside the submitted
112: primary article, this supplement, and the uploaded-or-equivalent review bundle
113: are treated below only as local historical or path-verified context unless a
114: displayed certificate explicitly places the corresponding record on the submitted
115: support surface.  The CICM presentation-only submission uses the short artifact
116: plus bibliography; this supplement and \path{review_bundle/} are
117: evidence-support materials rather than competing primary submissions.
118: 
119: \paragraph{Scope and theoremization boundary.}
120: The supplement is deliberately limited to auditable theory-to-paper workflow
121: claims for formal mathematics.  It does not propose a theorem prover, a Lean
122: hammer, a complete BEDC theory, an automated journal-acceptance method, or a
123: claim that AI output is proof evidence.  Some logistical facts are stated in
124: proposition or theorem environments because they are finite-record consequences
125: used by the certificate calculus; those statements should be read as conditional
126: audit-record claims over printed paths, tables, manifests, and logs.  The
127: mathematical obligations are discharged by the displayed TeX definitions,
128: theorems, and proofs in the core chain, not by the theorem inventory, upload
129: bookkeeping, digest records, or venue-boundary records.
130: 
131: \begin{proposition}[Final package-shape convention]
132: \label{prop:final-package-shape-convention}
133: For the current presentation package there is a single source-level package
134: shape.  The primary article is \path{submission_abstract.tex}; the selected
135: technical supplement is \path{main.tex}; the selected bounded theorem-obligation
136: ledger is \path{theorem_inventory.json}; and the selected supplemental support
137: surface is exact inclusion of \path{review_bundle/} with the final digest record
138: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  No archive locator, DOI-bearing
139: replacement, or alternate supplemental directory is part of the current package
140: convention.  Consequently every bundle-backed support claim in this source is
141: accepted only under exact inclusion of that directory with matching final digests;
142: without that external inclusion record it remains a local package-support claim.
143: \end{proposition}
144: 
145: \begin{proof}
146: The assertion is a finite record comparison.  The primary source names
147: \path{submission_abstract.tex} as the primary presentation article and states
148: that \path{main.tex}, \path{theorem_inventory.json}, and \path{review_bundle/}
149: are supplemental support surfaces.  The opening package paragraph of this
150: supplement names the same four coordinates and explicitly selects exact
151: supplemental inclusion rather than an archive replacement.  The digest manifest
152: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} records the same branch: its support
153: mode is conditional exact supplemental inclusion of \path{review_bundle/}, and
154: its archive-replacement fields state that no archive replacement or locator is
155: claimed.  These three finite source records therefore agree on one package
156: shape.
157: 
158: It remains only to identify the boundary of the convention.  A source-level
159: package-shape record fixes which files the manuscript treats as primary,
160: supplemental, and support material; it does not by itself prove that a venue
161: upload has occurred.  Hence bundle-backed claims are admissible externally only
162: after the selected exact support directory is actually supplied with the digests
163: listed in the final manifest.  If that external inclusion record is absent, the
164: finite coordinates still exist locally, but the submitted-support conclusion is
165: not instantiated.  Since the same finite records contain no archive locator, no
166: archive replacement can be inferred from the source.  This proves both the
167: single package convention and its conditional external boundary.
168: \end{proof}
169: 
170: \begin{theorem}[Nested six-row and seven-row package states]
171: \label{thm:nested-package-states}
172: Let $P^{+}_{6}$ denote the current row-frozen certificate state whose accepted
173: rows are exactly $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ in
174: Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger}.  Let $P^{+}_{7}$ denote the enlarged
175: submitted support state obtained by adjoining the single manual theorem-ledger
176: row $a_7$ whose submitted coordinate is \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  Then
177: $P^{+}_{6}\subset P^{+}_{7}$ as finite record surfaces, and the inclusion is
178: conservative for the first six certificates: every acceptance decision, evidence
179: level, assigned-gate family, discovery-interface field, and command-boundary
180: field of rows $a_1,\ldots,a_6$ is the same in $P^{+}_{6}$ and $P^{+}_{7}$.  The
181: only new accepted claim of $P^{+}_{7}$ is the bounded manual theorem-obligation
182: ledger claim for $a_7$.  In particular the two states are nested package states,
183: not competing final ledgers: $P^{+}_{6}$ is the exact six-row current-package
184: certificate state used by Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-certificate-exactness},
185: while $P^{+}_{7}$ is the submitted theorem-ledger extension used by
186: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-obligation-ledger-boundary}.
187: \end{theorem}
188: 
189: \begin{proof}
190: The tables and support records are finite, so the assertion is checked by
191: coordinates.  By definition, $P^{+}_{6}$ contains the primary article, this
192: supplement, the six accepted rows $a_1,\ldots,a_6$, their certificate fields, and
193: the conditional review-bundle support surface named in
194: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-package-acceptance-certificate-ledger}.  The state
195: $P^{+}_{7}$ is formed by adding one additional row, $a_7$, together with the
196: manifest exposure of \path{theorem_inventory.json}.  No row $a_i$ with
197: $1\leq i\leq 6$ is edited in this construction, and no field of its certificate
198: is replaced: the same finite tuple $(K_i,\ell_i,E_i,H_i,R_i,D_i,B_i)$ is read by
199: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table} in both states.  Hence
200: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness} gives the same acceptance decision
201: for each of the six rows in both states.
202: 
203: The added coordinate has a different declared role.  Its accepted claim is not a
204: new mathematical proof, a proof-assistant rebuild, or an extractor-completeness
205: claim; it is the manual obligation-ledger traceability claim later classified in
206: Proposition~\ref{prop:manual-theorem-inventory-boundary}.  Since the finite
207: record added to pass from $P^{+}_{6}$ to $P^{+}_{7}$ is exactly this new row and
208: its manifest exposure, every accepted claim outside $a_7$ is inherited unchanged
209: from $P^{+}_{6}$, while $a_7$ supplies only the bounded theorem-ledger coordinate.
210: This proves both the nesting and the non-competition statement.
211: \end{proof}
212: 
213: The setting is adjacent to proof-assistant systems such as Lean
214: \cite{deMouraKADR2015Lean}, machine-learning interfaces to formal proving such
215: as LeanDojo \cite{YangEtAl2023LeanDojo}, informal-to-formal guidance methods
216: such as Draft--Sketch--Prove \cite{JiangEtAl2023DraftSketchProve}, and
217: large-scale formal-proof archives such as the Archive of Formal Proofs
218: \cite{BlanchetteHMN2015AFP}.  Our focus is different: we study the
219: publication-facing evidence discipline around such sources.
